<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Smith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Smith Jr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Smith Jr. was an American religious and political leader, born in Sharon, Vermont, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mormonism">Mormonism</a> and the Latter Day Saint movement. He said that in 1823 an angel directed him to buried golden plates, and in 1830 he published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a> as their translation and organised the Church of Christ. His followers gathered in Kirtland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, and Independence, Missouri, and later founded Nauvoo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, which grew into the second-largest city in Illinois. In 1844, after he and the city council ordered the press of the Nauvoo Expositor destroyed, he surrendered to Illinois authorities and was killed by a mob that stormed the jailhouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-smith.jpg" alt="Joseph Smith" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Likely William Warner Major see · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Smith,_Jr._portrait_owned_by_Joseph_Smith_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Smith was born on 23 December 1805, one of eleven children of Joseph Sr. and Lucy Mack Smith. At seven he underwent painful surgery for a leg bone infection and walked with a slight limp afterwards. After years of crop failures the family lost its farm and travelled 300 miles west to Western New York. The Smiths engaged in folk magic, a common practice of the time and place, and hunted for buried treasure.</p><p>In 1838 Smith described a vision of 1820 in which God the Father and Jesus Christ appeared to him and told him all contemporary churches had turned aside from the gospel. According to the historian Steven C. Harper there is no evidence that he told anyone but a minister of it for at least a decade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1827 Smith and his wife Emma moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, where he dictated a translation of golden plates he said an angel had shown him. The first 116 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> pages, lent to Martin Harris, were lost. He began issuing revelations in July 1828, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a> was completed in 1829. Sidney Rigdon converted with over a hundred followers, and Smith moved to Kirtland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in January 1831.</p><p>In Missouri his followers were driven out of Independence, and in 1838 he declared Far West the new Zion. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> he renamed Commerce as Nauvoo, whose charter authorised the Nauvoo Legion, a militia he commanded. In April 1841 he secretly wed Louisa Beaman and in the next two and a half years married or was sealed to thirty or forty more women, a practice kept secret from most members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 1844 Smith secretly organised the Council of Fifty, which elected him Prophet, Priest and King. When dissenters published a paper that exposed him, he ordered their press destroyed, and he was shot dead by a mob that stormed the jail. Non-Mormon newspapers called him a fanatic, while Latter Day Saints saw a martyred prophet.</p><p>On 8 August most Latter Day Saints present elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> as successor, and they settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a>. Others followed Rigdon or James J. Strang, and in 1860 many joined under Joseph Smith III to form what is now the Community of Christ. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">LDS Church</a> regards Smith as a prophet on a par with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Smith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mormonism is the theology and religious tradition of the Latter Day Saint movement, a form of Restorationist Christianity started by <b>Joseph Smith</b> in Western New York in the 1820s and 1830s, during a period of religious excitement known as the Second Great Awakening.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Latter Day Saint church founded by <b>Joseph Smith</b>, informally organised in 1829 in upstate New York, was formally organised at Fayette or Manchester.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
